# Visual quality

- Visual direction must be demonstrated through rendered routes, screenshots, or selected references. Adjectives alone are not art direction.
- Match craft to `design_ambition` in `docs/quality-profile.md`. Studio and portfolio sites default to high visual impact unless the brief explicitly asks for restraint.
- High-ambition pages require one signature experience and up to two supporting interactions. Refine a few memorable moments rather than adding weak motion everywhere.
- Use gradients, glow, glass, sharp geometry, large radii, 3D, shaders, editorial type, or minimalism when they strengthen the chosen concept. Avoid them only when they are generic or incoherent—not by blanket prohibition.
- Do not repeat the same section composition more than twice consecutively. Normally use no more than one conventional card grid on a landing page.
- Portfolio claims require real screenshots, recordings, or clearly identified prototypes. Fabricated product UI cannot serve as proof of completed work.
- Phosphor is the utility icon family. Brand marks, hero illustrations, project diagrams, and signature visuals may use custom SVG, generated imagery, 3D, or bespoke iconography.
- Responsive design may change composition, crop, type scale, order, depth, and interaction—not merely stack desktop blocks.
- Every section must add a new fact, proof point, or action. Remove repetition during the editorial pass.
